  "meta_story": {
    "id": "quantum_physics_pioneers",
    "title": "Quantum’s First Architects",
    "tagline": "From quanta to paradox",
    "description": "Quantum physics didn’t arrive as a single discovery—it was assembled, piece by piece, from stubborn experimental facts and daring mathematical reconstructions of reality. In the early 20th century, new phenomena in radiation and matter forced physicists to abandon comfortable classical pictures. Soon, the argument shifted from “what works” to “what it means,” as probability, measurement, and uncertainty became features of nature rather than flaws in our instruments.\n\nThis meta-story follows a tightly connected chain of pioneers who each pushed on a different load-bearing wall of the emerging quantum framework. Marie Curie’s radioactivity opened a vista into the atom’s hidden interior; Albert Einstein’s light quanta and debates over indeterminacy made quantum theory unavoidable and controversial; Emmy Noether provided the symmetry logic that allowed the new physics to cohere; and John von Neumann supplied the rigorous mathematical architecture that turned quantum mechanics into a discipline with rules—and unsettling implications.",
    "person_ids": [
      "marie_curie",
      "albert_einstein",
      "emmy_noether",
      "john_von_neumann"
    ],
    "date_range_start": "1895",
    "date_range_end": "1957",
